      "text": "  This problem requires a deep understanding of the roots of unity and their properties. To solve this problem, we first need to recognize that the roots of the equation \\(z^{1997} - 1 = 0\\) are the 1997th roots of unity. These roots can be written as \\(e^{2\\pi i k / 1997}\\) for \\(k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 1996\\).\n\nGiven \\(v\\) and \\(w\\) are distinct, randomly chosen roots of unity, we need to find the probability that \\(\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}} \\le |v + w|\\).\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the magnitude\nThe magnitude of the sum \\(v + w\\) for \\(v = e^{2\\pi i k / 1997}\\) and \\(w = e^{2\\pi i m / 1997}\\) can be calculated using the properties of the roots of unity:\n\\[ |v + w| = \\left| e^{2\\pi i k / 1997} + e^{2\\pi i m / 1997} \\right|. \\]\n\n### Step 2: Simplifying the sum\nThe magnitude of the sum of two complex exponentials is given by:\n\\[ |e^{2\\pi i k / 1997} + e^{2\\pi i m / 1997}| = \\left| e^{2\\pi i (k - m) / 1997} (1 + e^{-2\\pi i (k - m) / 1997}) \\right|. \\]\nSince \\(|e^{2\\pi i \\alpha}| = 1\\) for any real \\(\\alpha\\), we have:\n\\[ |1 + e^{-2\\pi i (k - m) / 1997}| = \\sqrt{1 + 2\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi (k - m)}{1997}\\right) + 1} = \\sqrt{2 + 2\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi (k - m)}{1997}\\right)} = \\sqrt{2\\left(1 + \\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi (k - m)}{1997}\\right)\\right)}. \\]\nThus,\n\\[ |v + w| = \\sqrt{2\\left(1 + \\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi (k - m)}{1997}\\right)\\right)}. \\]\n\n### Step 3: Finding the range of \\(|v + w|\\)\nFor \\(k \\neq m\\), the expression \\(\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi (k - m)}{1997}\\right)\\) ranges from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\). Therefore,\n\\[ 1 \\leq |v + w| \\leq \\sqrt{4} = 2. \\]\n\n### Step 4: Determining the condition \\(\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}} \\le |v + w|\\)\nWe need to find the probability that:\n\\[ \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}} \\le 2. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ 2 \\le \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}}. \\]\nNotice that:\n\\[ \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}} \\approx \\sqrt{2 + 1.732} \\approx \\sqrt{3.732} \\approx 1.931. \\]\nSo, the condition \\(2 \\le \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}}\\) is always true. Therefore, \\(|v + w| \\le 2\\) for any distinct \\(v\\) and \\(w\\).\n\n### Step 5: Calculating the probability\nSince the condition \\(|v + w| \\le 2\\) is always satisfied for any distinct \\(v\\) and \\(w\\) when \\(k \\neq m\\), the probability is 1. Thus, the probability is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1}. \\]",
