      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by noting that the roots of the equation \\(z^{1997} - 1 = 0\\) are the 1997th roots of unity. These roots can be expressed as \\(z_k = e^{2\\pi i k / 1997}\\) for \\(k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 1996\\).\n\nThe magnitude of the sum of any two roots \\(z_v = e^{2\\pi i v / 1997}\\) and \\(z_w = e^{2\\pi i w / 1997}\\) is given by \\(|z_v + z_w|\\). Using the property of magnitudes of complex numbers, we have:\n\\[\n|z_v + z_w| = \\left| e^{2\\pi i v / 1997} + e^{2\\pi i w / 1997} \\right|\n\\]\nThis can be simplified using the geometric mean of the complex exponentials:\n\\[\n|z_v + z_w| = \\left| e^{2\\pi i v / 1997} (1 + e^{2\\pi i (w - v) / 1997}) \\right| = \\left| 1 + e^{2\\pi i (w - v) / 1997} \\right|\n\\]\nWe need to determine when this magnitude is less than or equal to \\(\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}}\\). This condition is equivalent to:\n\\[\n\\left| 1 + e^{2\\pi i (w - v) / 1997} \\right| \\leq \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\nThis inequality holds if and only if the point \\((1, 0)\\) on the complex plane is inside or on the boundary of the closed disk of radius \\(\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}}\\) centered at \\(e^{2\\pi i w / 1997}\\).\n\nThe vector \\(e^{2\\pi i (w - v) / 1997}\\) makes an angle \\(\\frac{2\\pi (w - v)}{1997}\\) with the positive real axis. The magnitude of this vector is the distance from the origin to the point \\(e^{2\\pi i v / 1997} + e^{2\\pi i w / 1997}\\), which is a point on the unit circle. For the point \\((1, 0)\\) to be inside or on this boundary, the line segment from \\(e^{2\\pi i v / 1997}\\) to \\(e^{2\\pi i w / 1997}\\) must cover at least the angle of \\(\\tan^{-1}(\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{3}} - 1)\\).\n\nThe angle \\(\\frac{2\\pi (w - v)}{1997}\\) must be such that the total angle covered by the path from \\(e^{2\\pi i v / 1997}\\) to \\(e^{2\\pi i w / 1997}\\) is at least \\(\\pi\\). This occurs when \\(w\\) and \\(v\\) are distinct roots and \\(w \\neq v + 2k\\) for any integer \\(k\\).\n\nThe total number of choices for \\(v\\) and \\(w\\) is \\(1997 \\times 1996\\). The number of valid pairs \\((v, w)\\) is \\(1996\\) (since \\(w\\) must be distinct from \\(v\\) and \\(v \\neq w + 2k\\)).\n\nThus, the probability is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1996}{1997 \\times 1996} = \\frac{1}{1997}\n\\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{1997}}\n\\]",
